## Knowledge Graph Terminology

The facility is held as a knowledge graph and you answer by writing read-only
Cypher. There is no level-by-level navigation and no fixed lookup call: devices,
the classes they belong to, the sections they sit in and the PVs bound to each
of them are nodes and relationships, and a query walks between them.
{%- else %}
## Channel Database Terminology

### Database Profile

This is a **graph** channel database — the facility is held as a knowledge graph
and you reach addresses by writing read-only Cypher. There is no level-by-level
navigation and no fixed lookup call: devices, the classes they belong to, the
sections they sit in and the PVs bound to each of them are nodes and
relationships, and a query walks between them.
{%- endif %}

### Node Types and Predicates

The corpus is RDF loaded through neosemantics, so the spellings follow the
source ontology rather than any convention you would invent. `get_schema()` is
authoritative; this table is the orientation.

| Shape | What it is |
|---|---|
| `(d:Resource)` | Every node carries `Resource`. A device carries it plus one concrete class label (`Quadrupole`, `BeamPositionMonitor`, …) — there is **no** `Device` label, so match devices as `:Resource` plus properties |
| `(b:ChannelBinding)` | One PV bound to one device. `b.fullPv` is the address you return |
| `(c:Class)` | An ontology class. `c.uri` identifies it, `c.altLabel` holds its synonyms |
| `(d)-[:HASBINDING]->(b)` | Device to binding — the hop from a device to its addresses |
| `(b)-[:READSSIGNAL]->()` / `(b)-[:WRITESSIGNAL]->()` | Binding to semantic signal. Which one the binding has is the read/write direction |
| `(d)-[:TYPE]->(c)` / `(c)-[:SUBCLASSOF]->(parent)` | Device to class, class to parent class. `SUBCLASSOF*0..` rolls a whole subtree up |

Relationship types are UPPERCASED by the loader. Writing `hasBinding` matches
nothing and returns zero rows rather than an error.

Properties worth knowing:

- **On a device:** `sourceName`, `sectionCode`, `system`, `sPositionM`,
  `ordinalInSection`, plus the free text `familyDescription`,
  `systemDescription`, `ringDescription`
- **On a binding:** `fullPv`, `confidence`, plus the free text `description`,
  `fieldDescription`, `subfieldDescription`
- **On a class:** `uri`, `altLabel`

`altLabel` is a **list**, always — even where a class has one synonym. Match it
with `ANY(l IN c.altLabel WHERE toLower(l) CONTAINS $term)`, never with `=`.

### Address Grammar

`ChannelBinding.fullPv` is the address, whole and already formatted for the
control system. Return it verbatim: never assemble an address out of
`sectionCode`, `sourceName` and a signal name, and never adjust one you were
given. Two devices can share a `sourceName` in different sections, so a device
is identified by `sourceName` **and** `sectionCode` together.

### Terminology Mapping

Operators use domain language. Map their terms to a graph query:

| Operator Language | Graph Query |
|---|---|
| "beam current" | Search binding descriptions for `current`; or class `altLabel` `beam current monitor`, `dcct`, `bc` |
| "BPM positions" / "beam position" | Class `BeamPositionMonitor` via `altLabel` `beam position monitor`, `bpm` → `HASBINDING` → bindings whose `description`/`subfieldDescription` names the `X`/`Y` axis |
| "corrector magnets" | Class `Corrector` (or `HCorrector`/`VCorrector`) with `SUBCLASSOF*0..` so both planes roll up |
| "quadrupole" / "quad" | Class `Quadrupole` via `altLabel` |
| "all magnets" | `(:Class)-[:SUBCLASSOF*0..]->(:Class {uri: $magnet_uri})` — name the parent class and let the hierarchy supply the kinds |
| "readback" / "monitor" | Bindings with a `READSSIGNAL` edge and no `WRITESSIGNAL` |
| "setpoint" / "control" | Bindings with a `WRITESSIGNAL` edge |
| "in sector 3" / "in the storage ring" | `d.sectionCode` for the location, `d.system` for the accelerator system |
| "in beamline order" | Order by `d.sPositionM`, falling back to `d.ordinalInSection` |
| "what is this PV" | Reverse lookup: `MATCH (d:Resource)-[:HASBINDING]->(b:ChannelBinding {fullPv: $pv})` |

### Graph Conventions

- Start every unfamiliar question at `example_queries()` — the examples are
  written against the corpus that is actually loaded, so adapting one is
  reliable where inventing a query shape is guesswork
- Pass every value through `params` as a `$name` placeholder; a value pasted
  into the query text is both a correctness and a safety problem
- End every query with a `LIMIT`, including aggregates — bounded results are the
  contract. The `LIMIT` is a guard, not a target: set it above the number of
  rows the question implies, and if a result still comes back truncated, narrow
  the query rather than reporting the partial list as the whole answer
- A query that matches a device has not yet answered the question. The device's
  other bindings stay out of the answer unless the question reaches them, and
  the same is true of the same device in a section the question did not name
- Write the `WHERE` clause so it carries every filter the question stated —
  location, device identity AND kind of signal. A query filtered only down to
  the device returns that device's whole binding list, which is how a precise
  question comes back with a padded answer
- "All" widens the number of rows, never the kind of row: a question asking for
  all of one kind of signal wants every binding of that kind, and none of the
  other kinds that sit beside it
- Free-text descriptions are richer than names, but they are corpus-dependent: a
  facility export may carry none. A zero-row description search means fall back
  to the name and class route, not that the device is absent
- Match text case-insensitively (`toLower(...) CONTAINS $term`); the corpus
  spells names in the facility's own casing
- A misspelled label or property returns **zero rows, not an error** — when a
  query surprises you with an empty result, check the spelling with
  `get_schema()` before concluding the facility has no such device
